1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,202
Decibels exist because radio numbers are ridiculous.

2
00:00:05,202 --> 00:00:10,129
The signal your antenna picks up from a repeater ten miles away can be a hundred

3
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:14,625
billion times weaker than the signal leaving that repeater's transmitter.

4
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:17,211
Nobody wants to do math with eleven zeros.

5
00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:22,385
The decibel is how radio engineers make those numbers small, friendly — and addable.

6
00:00:22,835 --> 00:00:28,007
A decibel is a logarithmic way of writing a ratio between two power levels.

7
00:00:28,007 --> 00:00:29,663
Not an amount — a ratio.

8
00:00:29,663 --> 00:00:35,180
Ten decibels means "ten times the power." Twenty decibels means a hundred times.

9
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:36,836
Thirty means a thousand.

10
00:00:36,836 --> 00:00:40,767
Every ten dB you add multiplies the power by another ten.

11
00:00:41,217 --> 00:00:43,302
The everyday anchor is a piano.

12
00:00:43,302 --> 00:00:48,886
Each octave is the same-sized step to your ear, but each one doubles the frequency.

13
00:00:48,886 --> 00:00:54,066
Decibels do that for power: equal steps that stand for equal multiplications.

14
00:00:54,066 --> 00:00:59,314
Your ear and your radio both live on that kind of scale, which is why dB feels

15
00:00:59,314 --> 00:01:00,861
natural once it clicks.

16
00:01:01,311 --> 00:01:04,147
Three numbers are worth memorizing.

17
00:01:04,147 --> 00:01:06,741
Plus three dB: double the power.

18
00:01:06,741 --> 00:01:08,442
Minus three dB: half.

19
00:01:08,442 --> 00:01:10,063
Plus ten: ten times.

20
00:01:10,063 --> 00:01:13,386
That's it — every other value is a combo.

21
00:01:13,386 --> 00:01:14,277
Sixteen dB?

22
00:01:14,277 --> 00:01:19,707
That's 10 + 3 + 3: ten times two times two — forty times the power.

23
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:24,083
You just did logarithms in your head without noticing.

24
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:28,488
One footnote for the meter-readers: those rules are for power.

25
00:01:28,488 --> 00:01:33,656
When the quantity is a voltage, a current, or a field strength, power goes as the

26
00:01:33,656 --> 00:01:38,760
square of the amplitude, so the same ratio is written with twenty log instead of

27
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:39,015
ten.

28
00:01:39,015 --> 00:01:44,183
It's not a different decibel — it falls straight out of the squaring — but it's a

29
00:01:44,183 --> 00:01:49,479
classic source of two-times errors: a voltage that doubles is plus six dB, not plus

30
00:01:49,479 --> 00:01:49,861
three.

31
00:01:50,311 --> 00:01:55,160
And here's the superpower: on a log scale, multiplication becomes addition.

32
00:01:55,160 --> 00:02:00,332
Follow a signal from transmitter to receiver — every cable loses a few dB, every

33
00:02:00,332 --> 00:02:04,922
antenna adds a few, the path between them takes away a hundred or more.

34
00:02:04,922 --> 00:02:10,158
Instead of multiplying raw numbers twelve orders of magnitude apart, you just add

35
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:12,680
and subtract small ones down the chain.

36
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:18,045
That running sum is called a link budget, and it's how every radio link on Earth is

37
00:02:18,045 --> 00:02:18,627
designed.

38
00:02:19,077 --> 00:02:19,698
One trap.

39
00:02:19,698 --> 00:02:23,766
A plain dB is always relative — a ratio between two things.

40
00:02:23,766 --> 00:02:29,144
The moment you see a letter after it, it's become an absolute number, measured

41
00:02:29,144 --> 00:02:34,454
against a fixed reference: dB-m means compared to one milliwatt; dB-F-S means

42
00:02:34,454 --> 00:02:38,177
compared to the loudest sample your SDR can represent.

43
00:02:38,177 --> 00:02:40,108
Same math, different anchor.

44
00:02:40,558 --> 00:02:42,839
And each anchor has its own quirk.

45
00:02:42,839 --> 00:02:48,342
Received radio signals are tiny fractions of a milliwatt, so they land as negative

46
00:02:48,342 --> 00:02:53,844
dBm — and less negative means stronger: minus seventy beats minus ninety by twenty

47
00:02:53,844 --> 00:02:56,461
dB, which is a hundred times the power.

48
00:02:56,461 --> 00:03:01,561
On the dBFS scale, zero is the ceiling — the largest value the converter can

49
00:03:01,561 --> 00:03:07,130
represent — so real samples are always negative, and driving the input to zero dBFS

50
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:08,137
means clipping.

51
00:03:08,587 --> 00:03:13,605
Decibels are also what let one screen hold that whole absurd range: a spectrum

52
00:03:13,605 --> 00:03:18,881
display marked in dB shows a noise floor near minus one-twenty and a strong signal

53
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:23,707
at minus twenty on the same plot — a linear scale could never resolve both.

54
00:03:23,707 --> 00:03:28,983
And you'll see all three flavors in GopherTrunk's dashboard: signal level in dBFS,

55
00:03:28,983 --> 00:03:33,358
signal-to-noise ratio in plain dB, and hardware specs quoted in dBm.

56
00:03:33,358 --> 00:03:37,862
Now they're not three mysteries — they're one idea with three anchors.

57
00:03:38,312 --> 00:03:43,962
So: decibels turn absurd ratios into small numbers, multiplication into addition,

58
00:03:43,962 --> 00:03:47,450
and three memorized steps into fluent mental math.

59
00:03:47,450 --> 00:03:51,217
The full write-up, with the formulas, is linked below.

